import "core"
import "core"


# A guard on an arm of a [case] or a [match] selects that arm only when its condition holds.
  This is a regression test for a gap that made both forms answer with the guarded arm whether or
  not its guard was true: @abs(x= -5)@ answered @-5@, having run the arm guarded by @x2 >= 0@ on an
  input that is not.
  The cause was where the guard was put. A branch preprocessor
  (@Syntax/Ct/Desugarf/Preprocessors.hs@) rewrites each arm into an arm of a generated @$hash@
  function, and it used to leave the arm's guard inside the pattern nested in that function's
  argument. @Semantics/exprWhereConds@ reads only an [AliasExpr]'s base, so a guard sitting there
  was invisible to it — and that is the field both @TreeBuild/completeTreeSet@ and
  @TreeBuild/buildGuards@ read to decide whether an arm is guarded. Reading as unconditional, the
  arm's domain covered the whole source on its own, so its siblings were dropped as adding nothing
  and the lone survivor was then run without its condition ever being evaluated. The guard is now
  hoisted onto the generated arm as a whole.
  The equivalent choice written as guards on a declared function's own arms was always correct, and
  @test/Integration/code/cond.ct@ covers that form.
  A test of a branch has to run the branch that is not taken: @case.ct@ and @match.ct@ missed this
  because each calls @abs(0)@ and nothing else, and at zero both arms answer zero.
abs(x -> Integer)= case(x)
  x2 | x2 >= 0= x2
  x2= x2 * -1

> abs(x= 5)
  >>> 5
> abs(x= -5)
  >>> 5
main{io -> IO}= io.exit(val= 0)
